# Onni Work environment variables — copy to `.env` and adjust. Every var is optional;
# the defaults give a zero-config local cockpit. Onni Work reads these from the process
# environment (it does not auto-load `.env` — export them, or use your shell/dotenv).

# ---- deployment mode --------------------------------------------------------
# Local (default) exposes host-only conveniences: "open in editor/Finder/terminal",
# worktree-path handoff, the launch bookmarklet. Set to 1 for VPS / hosted mode to
# hide those host-machine affordances (the cockpit is being viewed over the network).
# The Add project browser is always confined to ONNI_BROWSE_ROOT (default `~/`); set that
# root narrowly for a hosted cockpit if the remote viewer should not enumerate the whole home.
# ONNI_REMOTE=1
# ONNI_INSTANCE_ID is set automatically by server-install in generated services; do not edit.

# ---- Design Mode (spec .ai/specs/2026-10-09-design-mode.md) ------------------
# A task's Preview tab can pick an element of the task's app and send a note about it to the
# agent. To see into the framed page, Onni Work re-serves that dev server on a second 127.0.0.1 port
# with a small picker script injected — opened only when you switch Design Mode on, loopback only,
# and only for a local http:// address. ON on a local cockpit, always off on a hosted one. Set to
# 0 to remove the toggle and make the route refuse.
# ONNI_DESIGN_MODE=0

# Allow a remote cockpit to list and manage host-side agent account folders. This exposes absolute
# paths and account identity, so enable it only behind an authenticated reverse proxy. Account
# folders are confined to ONNI_BROWSE_ROOT. It does not enable local desktop actions; Connect
# returns a command to run on the host.
# ONNI_REMOTE_AGENT_ACCOUNTS=1

# ---- run history ------------------------------------------------------------
# How many settled runs the index keeps per project, at least 1 (live runs and runs pinned by an
# active dispatch tree are never pruned).
# ONNI_RUNS_KEEP=300
# ONNI_ARCHIVED_RUNS_KEEP=500

# ---- dev launcher (scripts/dev.mjs) -----------------------------------------
# API port the cockpit server listens on and Vite proxies /api to. `npm run dev`
# probes for a free port and sets this automatically; set it only to pin a port.
# ONNI_API_PORT=4321

# ---- agent CLIs -------------------------------------------------------------
# Override the discovered path to each backend CLI (defaults: found on PATH).
# claude is also looked for where its own installers put it when PATH does not name it —
# ~/.local/bin, ~/.claude/local, /opt/homebrew/bin, /usr/local/bin — so an Onni Work started from an
# IDE or a launcher finds a native-installer claude without this override.
# ONNI_CLAUDE_BIN=/usr/local/bin/claude
# ONNI_CODEX_BIN=/usr/local/bin/codex
# ONNI_OPENCODE_BIN=/usr/local/bin/opencode
# Unset, Cursor resolves to `cursor-agent` on PATH, then ~/.local/bin/cursor-agent, then `agent`.
# ONNI_CURSOR_AGENT_BIN=/usr/local/bin/cursor-agent
# ONNI_PI_BIN=/usr/local/bin/pi
# ONNI_JUNIE_BIN=/usr/local/bin/junie
# ONNI_COPILOT_BIN=/usr/local/bin/copilot

# The agents' OWN home variables (not Onni Work's) are honoured where the vendor documents one:
# setting one here moves that agent's DEFAULT account — the one Onni Work discovers. A SECOND login of
# the same CLI is deliberately NOT configured through the environment, because a process-wide
# variable cannot differ per project: add it in Settings → Agent accounts and pick it per project,
# and the two accounts can then be used side by side.
# CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR=~/.claude
# CODEX_HOME=~/.codex

# Claude denies tools that require approval instead of interrupting a headless run. Set to 1
# to select Claude's `acceptEdits` mode. Onni Work has no cockpit permission response channel,
# so this does not make approval prompts actionable in the cockpit.
# ONNI_APPROVAL_GATE=1

# Codex runs with full tool access by default. Set to 0 to opt into its network-blocked
# workspace-write sandbox.
# ONNI_CODEX_NETWORK=0

# Agents get a least-privilege environment by default (toolchain vars, the backend's own auth,
# GITHUB_TOKEN, ONNI_*). Name extra host variables to forward, or set ONNI_AGENT_ENV_FULL=1 to hand
# agents the whole host environment, every secret in it included.
# ONNI_ENV_PASSTHROUGH=A,B
# ONNI_AGENT_ENV_FULL=1

# Recognized credentials and token patterns are scrubbed from saved run events and logs.
# Set to 0 to store them unredacted.
# ONNI_REDACT_SECRETS=0

# ---- project folders --------------------------------------------------------
# Zero-config defaults for the two independent Add project folders. Saved values in Global
# Settings → Projects override these defaults. The browse root must already exist; missing
# checkout folders are created recursively when saved and when a clone starts.
# ONNI_BROWSE_ROOT=~/
# ONNI_PROJECTS_DIR=~/onni/projects

# ---- local state and startup UI ---------------------------------------------
# Override Onni Work's global state directory (default: `~/.onni`). Useful for isolated test or
# container installs; an empty value deliberately falls back to the default. This home holds
# `config.json` — the per-user workspace config (parallel cap, memory ceiling, project roots)
# and the project registry every repo Onni Work boots in registers itself into — so two cockpits
# started with different ONNI_HOME values serve disjoint sets of projects. Missing, corrupt or
# read-only: Onni Work falls back to defaults and keeps running.
# ONNI_HOME=/path/to/onni-state

# Keep the secret store's data key out of the OS keychain. By default Onni Work encrypts
# `~/.onni/secrets/*.json` with one per-machine key held in the OS keychain (macOS Keychain,
# Linux Secret Service, Windows Credential Manager; optional `@napi-rs/keyring`) and falls back
# to a `0600` key file beside the secrets when no keychain is usable. Set to 0 to use the key
# file only — a headless box whose keychain prompts, or a test install. Only the exact value 0.
# ONNI_SECRETS_KEYCHAIN=0

# Marks a supervised launch: the desktop shell sets it beside ONNI_SUPERVISOR_PID so Onni Work can
# tell it was started by the shell rather than from a terminal. Off by default.
# ONNI_SUPERVISED=1
# Set by the desktop shell (packages/desktop) on the Onni Work it spawns — never set these by hand:
# ONNI_DESKTOP=1 marks the process, ONNI_SUPERVISOR_PID names the shell so Onni Work exits when it dies.
# ONNI_DISCLAIM_EXEC (macOS) is the shell's own executable: Onni Work starts agent backends through it
# so privacy prompts name the agent (`claude`, `codex`, …) instead of Onni Work. Unset: plain spawn.
# ONNI_DESKTOP=1
# ONNI_SUPERVISOR_PID=12345
# ONNI_DISCLAIM_EXEC=/Applications/Onni Work.app/Contents/MacOS/onni-desktop
# Desktop shell knobs (read by the shell itself, not by Onni Work): run a checkout's build instead
# of the one linked under ~/.onni/versions, or boot into a given folder.
# ONNI_DESKTOP_ENTRY=/path/to/packages/work/dist/index.js
# ONNI_DESKTOP_CWD=/path/to/repo
# ONNI_DESKTOP_NODE=/opt/homebrew/bin/node   # the node the shell runs (default: the login shell's `node`)
# New Task defaults. Exact 0/1 only; workspace Settings overrides these seeds.
# ONNI_AUTONOMOUS_DEFAULT=0
# ONNI_WORKTREE_DEFAULT=1

# Dangerous escape hatch for any run executing in the repository root, including an
# explicit worktree opt-out, non-Git degradation, or a continuation whose worktree
# cannot be restored. Only the exact value 1 bypasses the repository-root lease.
# Concurrent agents may overwrite each other's files or Git state; isolated worktrees
# are unaffected. Off by default.
# ONNI_DISABLE_REPO_LOCK=1

# Opt into a launch-project-only cockpit. Only the exact value 1 enables this mode
# (`true`, `yes`, and every other value leave the default multi-project workspace on).
# While enabled, Onni Work exposes only the repo it was launched in and refuses project add,
# edit, checkout, browse, and remove operations. Registry rows are retained: unset the
# variable and restart to restore the full workspace without migration or data loss.
# ONNI_SINGLE_PROJECT=1

# Hide token counts or backend-reported cost independently throughout the browser
# cockpit. The legacy ONNI_HIDE_TOKEN_METRICS master switch hides both. Only the exact
# value 1 enables any presentation-only mode; telemetry and API payloads are preserved.
# Restart Onni Work after changing these flags.
# ONNI_HIDE_TOKEN_USAGE=1
# ONNI_HIDE_COST=1
# ONNI_HIDE_TOKEN_METRICS=1

# ---- task terminal (spec .ai/specs/2026-10-07-task-workspace.md §6) ---------
# An interactive shell in a task's worktree, from the cockpit. Unset: on for a local cockpit, off
# on a hosted one (ONNI_REMOTE=1 or a non-loopback bind) — Onni Work has no sign-in of its own there.
# 1 opts a hosted cockpit in (anyone your proxy admits gets a shell); 0 turns it off everywhere.
# A platform with no prebuilt PTY binary reports the terminal unavailable instead of failing.
# ONNI_TERMINAL=1

# ---- follow-up inbox --------------------------------------------------------
# Off by default. Set to 1 to turn the global follow-up inbox back on: agents are then asked
# to leave follow-ups in `.ai/onni/todos.json` when they finish, the Inbox view reappears in
# the cockpit, and the composer offers a per-task toggle. Left off, agents are never asked and
# the Inbox is hidden — the per-task handoff journal ("Notes") is a separate feature and always
# runs. Existing todos.json entries are kept either way, so this is safe to flip back and forth.
# ONNI_FOLLOWUPS=1
# The browser suite exercises the default-off route. To also exercise the enabled inbox in a
# fresh shared test environment, run: `ONNI_FOLLOWUPS=1 npm run test:e2e -- --force`.

# ---- automations ------------------------------------------------------------
# On by default (spec .ai/specs/2026-09-14-automations-redesign.md): the Automations view lists
# GitHub-triggered and scheduled automations, and Onni Work polls GitHub or fires schedules on each
# ENABLED automation while it is running. Nothing polls and nothing launches until you enable an
# automation yourself. A running task may also create automations from a prompt (spec
# .ai/specs/2026-09-13-automations-from-prompt.md): the built-in `create-onni-automation` skill
# is listed, every task's system prompt names the `onni automation` CLI, and the composer offers
# the "Create an automation" template. Set to 0 to opt out entirely: the nav item disappears, the
# endpoints answer 409, the workspace scheduler never starts, and none of the three appear. Only
# the exact value 0 opts out (`ONNI_AUTOMATIONS=1`, the old opt-in, is accepted and changes
# nothing), and it is read at boot — set it and restart Onni Work. Definitions, receipts and their
# frozen high-watermarks are kept either way, so this is safe to flip back and forth.
# ONNI_AUTOMATIONS=0
# The browser suite exercises the default-on route. To exercise the opted-out route in a fresh
# shared test environment, run: `ONNI_AUTOMATIONS=0 npm run test:e2e -- --force`.

# ---- dispatch ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Task dispatch (spec .ai/specs/2026-09-10-dispatch.md): a running task may dispatch other Onni Work
# tasks with `onni task create` (each in its own worktree forked off the parent's branch, with a
# budget carved out of the parent's) and report back with `onni task report`. ON by default — the
# owner-approved exception to "cost-widening features are opt-in" (AGENTS.md § Zero config): the
# brakes are in the engine (four children in flight, a child's budget carved out of its parent's),
# and a default-off dispatch sent the agent through its own sub-agents instead, unbudgeted and
# invisible. Only the exact value `0` turns it off; read at boot, so restart after changing it.
# Off, the `/runs/:id/{dispatch,report}` routes answer 409, no task's system prompt mentions
# dispatching, and the cockpit hides the "Review open PRs" template.
# ONNI_DISPATCH=0

# ---- run limits and unattended safety ---------------------------------------
# No environment variable on purpose. The cost / token / time / step ceilings, the unanswered-wait
# expiry and dispatch's depth / subtask / retry caps are Settings → Limits (stored in
# ~/.onni/config.json), because "Unlimited" must be a deliberate, confirmed choice and never a
# flag a shell profile can flip. `onni run --no-limit | --limit …` and `onni stop --all` are CLI flags.

# ---- agent temp directory ---------------------------------------------------
# Every run gets TMPDIR/TEMP/TMP pointing at `.ai/onni/tmp/<task-id>`, created and write-probed
# before the agent spawns (#785), so concurrent tasks stop sharing one directory.
# Only an exact `0` turns the whole mechanism off, pre-spawn check included.
# ONNI_AGENT_TMPDIR=0

# ---- leftover processes -----------------------------------------------------
# When a task settles, Onni Work stops every process still carrying the task's ONNI_TASK_ID in its
# environment — a hung test binary, a dev server, a `setsid`ed background job (#1411): SIGTERM
# five seconds after the settle, SIGKILL three seconds later. On boot it does the same for tasks
# that settled while it was down. Linux and macOS only. Only the exact value `0` turns it off.
# ONNI_TASK_PROCESS_REAP=0

# Workflow webhooks (spec 2026-09-30-workflow-node-editor): lets a workflow graph's `notify.webhook`
# node POST to the URL it names. OFF by default — a workflow file (which may come from another repo)
# calling out to an arbitrary URL widens exposure, so it is opt-in. Off, the node takes its `failed`
# port with a note saying how to turn it on; under ONNI_DRY_RUN=1 nothing is sent either way.
# ONNI_WORKFLOW_WEBHOOKS=1

# Project lifecycle webhook receivers in Settings. Opt-in; no capture, delivery or
# webhook state when off. Independent of ONNI_WORKFLOW_WEBHOOKS (graph notify steps).
# ONNI_WEBHOOKS=1

# ---- task auto-naming ---------------------------------------------------------
# Short LLM-generated task titles (spec 2026-07-17-task-auto-naming). On by default.
# ONNI_TITLE_UPDATES=0 turns off the live per-turn title refresh (the Settings → Agents
# toggle, when set, wins over this env default). ONNI_AUTONAME=0 is the kill-switch for
# ALL LLM naming — creation-time and live — leaving only the heuristic titles. Under
# ONNI_DRY_RUN=1 naming is off by default (mock titles would clobber honest heuristics);
# ONNI_AUTONAME=1 forces it there — used by the test suite.
# ONNI_TITLE_UPDATES=0
# ONNI_AUTONAME=0

# ---- review gate -------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional diff-first review gate (#489). OFF by default: a successful run with
# changes settles straight to `done`, leaving the diff in the worktree. Set
# ONNI_REVIEW_GATE=1 to make a changed non-autonomous run park at `review` for a
# human to Accept / Send back / open a Draft PR. Only the exact string "1"
# enables it ("true"/"yes" are treated as off). The Settings → Agents toggle,
# when set, wins over this env default. Autonomous runs ALWAYS skip the gate.
# ONNI_REVIEW_GATE=1

# ---- periodic autosave -------------------------------------------------------
# Off by default. Set to 1 to re-enable the janitor-style "onni autosave" commit every 90 s
# in each task worktree (spec 006). Left off, a task branch carries only the agent's own
# commits — plus the turn-end and pre-PR flushes, which always run so the branch still ends
# holding the finished state and the diff/review/PR views stay complete. Those flushes commit
# as `onni autosave (turn end|run finalize|pre-PR)`; only `(periodic)` is this flag's timer.
# ONNI_AUTOSAVE=1

# ---- GitHub -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Used for the GitHub tab / PR actions. The `gh` CLI's own auth is preferred; set
# this only if you drive git/gh through a token instead.
# GITHUB_TOKEN=ghp_xxx

# ---- external issue trackers ------------------------------------------------
# Jira/Linear credentials are configured per project in Settings → Issue tracker.
# The form writes managed <project-path-hash>.env files under ONNI_HOME/tracker-connections
# (outside repositories by default), with owner-only POSIX permissions; plaintext, not encrypted.
# JIRA_BASE_URL/JIRA_EMAIL/JIRA_API_TOKEN/LINEAR_API_KEY are fields in those files only:
# putting them here or exporting them into the server environment has no effect.
# No global dotenv loading or shared-credential fallback. Replace tokens through Settings.
# Existing environment-based connections must be explicitly reconnected in Settings.

# ---- cockpit bundle (build time) --------------------------------------------
# Where the cockpit's JS should look for the API. Empty (the default) means "the origin that
# served this page", and that is right in BOTH normal cases: the bundled cockpit is served by the
# CLI itself, and `npm run dev` proxies `/api` (including the `/api/v1/ws` upgrade) from the Vite
# dev server to the local service — so a relative path already reaches localhost either way, and
# setting this in development would only bypass the proxy.
# Set it only when the bundle is deployed apart from the service it talks to. A deployment that
# must be configured without a rebuild can instead put `<meta name="onni-api-base" content="…">`
# in the served HTML, which wins over this.
# VITE_ONNI_API_BASE=http://localhost:4321

# ---- testing / internal -----------------------------------------------------
# Run agents against a mock CLI (no real model calls) — used by the test suite.
# ONNI_DRY_RUN=1

# Lock model selection to the defaults in native Claude/Codex/OpenCode settings.
# Existing `.ai/onni/config.json` model presets are preserved but ignored while locked.
# `"modelsLocked": true` in global `~/.onni/config.json` or a repository's
# `.ai/onni/config.json` enables the same policy.
# Exact `1` also delegates provider authentication/enablement to the native
# agents: Onni Work skips its credential probes and provider-disable preferences,
# while Claude/Codex/OpenCode runner selection remains available.
# ONNI_AGENT_MODELS_LOCKED=1
# ONNI_MOCK_ARGS_FILE=/tmp/onni-mock-args.json
# Scale the mock Claude CLI's cosmetic pauses between events (default 1, paced for the demo
# cockpit; 0 = none). The vitest setup pins 0; `mock:pause`/`mock:slow` never scale.
# ONNI_MOCK_PACE=1
# How far out the mock CLI's `mock:limit` reply puts its usage-limit reset (seconds, default 60),
# so a ONNI_DRY_RUN=1 session can exercise auto-resume without waiting for a real window.
# ONNI_MOCK_LIMIT_RESET_SECONDS=60
# Make the mock CLI answer `--resume <id>` with "No conversation found" for these session ids
# (comma-separated, `*` = every resume), like a transcript Claude pruned after cleanupPeriodDays.
# ONNI_MOCK_MISSING_SESSIONS=*
# Make the bundled junie ACP mock reject `authenticate` with this message, so a ONNI_DRY_RUN=1
# session can exercise junie's auth-failure card without a real logged-out CLI.
# ONNI_MOCK_JUNIE_AUTH_ERROR="Authentication required"
# The following are set by Onni Work for child agent processes; you should not set them:
#   ONNI_TASK_ID, ONNI_HANDOFF_FILE, ONNI_TODOS_FILE
#   — and, while dispatch is on (not ONNI_DISPATCH=0): ONNI_API_URL (this cockpit's address), ONNI_PROJECT_ID (the
#   run's project) and ONNI_BIN (this cockpit's own entrypoint, so `node "$ONNI_BIN" task …`
#   never hits an older `onni` on the PATH), which the `onni task`, `onni automation` and `onni runs` CLIs use; and, for a run in a dispatch tree only,
#   ONNI_TREE_DIR: the tree directory under .ai/onni/dispatch/<rootRunId>/ (brief.md,
#   units/<id8>/{order,notes,report}.md, inbox/<id8>/) that every task of the tree reads and
#   writes as its file channel.
# The exception is a person at a shell: to use those CLIs outside a task, set ONNI_API_URL (and
# ONNI_PROJECT_ID on a multi-project cockpit) in that shell only, e.g.
#   ONNI_API_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4321 onni runs list
# Never put them in the cockpit's own environment; it sets ONNI_API_URL itself at boot.
# Set by Onni Work for workflow CHECK steps (spec .ai/specs/2026-10-06-agentic-e2e-checks.md);
# do not set them. A variable a run does not have is absent, never empty, and an inherited
# value is replaced, never passed through:
#   ONNI_RUN_ID, ONNI_PROJECT_ID, ONNI_WORKTREE (= the check's cwd), ONNI_BRANCH, ONNI_BASE (the
#   recorded fork point), ONNI_STEP_ID, ONNI_ATTEMPT (1-based, per check step),
#   ONNI_SHARED_CACHE_DIR (~/.onni/cache/<projectId>, 0700, shared by the project's tasks);
#   GitHub automation runs only: ONNI_GITHUB_REPO, ONNI_GITHUB_NUMBER, ONNI_GITHUB_EVENT;
#   pull-request-head runs only (automation `checkout: "pr-head"`): ONNI_PR_HEAD_SHA, ONNI_PR_HEAD_REF, ONNI_PR_BASE_REF.
# One exception: ONNI_PROJECT_ID is also an INPUT (it is how `onni task` / `onni automation` address
# a cockpit), so it is replaced, never cleared. A headless `onni run` has no project of its own
# and forwards whatever you exported, so a check step still reaches the cockpit you named.
